  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1057 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThursday through Monday:\n\nBehind the cold front, high pressure is expected to briefly build\ninto the Great Lakes Region bringing quiet weather for Thursday.\nLight and variable winds, mostly clear skies and highs in the\nupper 70s to low 80s Thursday. As the high pressure moves out of\nthe area heading into Friday, south to southwest winds will return\nand so to will the WAA. Hot and humid conditions are expected to\nreturn to the state with highs in the 80s and dewpoints expected\nto climb back into the 60s. A weak cold front is expected to move\nacross the state which will bring a low chance (10-20%) for rain\nand a storm or two for Friday night. \n\nHeading into the weekend, zonal flow is expected to set up across\nthe northern US, including the Great Lakes Region. This puts\nWisconsin in a situation where multiple shortwave troughs could\nmove through the larger scale flow. This unsettle pattern is\nfairly consistent across guidance, however when the shortwaves\nmove through and the strength of each varies. Rain and storms\nchances will be persistent Saturday afternoon through early next\nweek. Monday looks to have the best chances for rain around\n40-70% otherwise the rest of the precip chances are around \n20-40%. As we get closer in time to this unsettled period, the\nrain chances should become more defined in time, duration and\nintensity. Not anticipating any all day rains at this point.\nUncertainty remains for any stronger to severe storms as well.\nSome guidance wants to hit at stronger storms, but these same runs\nare the ones that want to hit at potential heat indices in the \n90s. Stuck to the average among guidance for the forecast given \nall the uncertainties.\n\nPatterson",
